<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keith Holyoake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1983">1983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Keith Holyoake</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Keith Holyoake was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> farmer and politician of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> who was Prime Minister briefly in 1957 and again from 1960 to 1972, one of the longest tenures in the country's history. His government presided over prosperity, sent forces to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> and prepared for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>. He later served as Governor-General from 1977 to 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keith-holyoake.jpg" alt="Keith Holyoake" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: S P Andrew Ltd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Keith_Holyoake_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Holyoake was born at Mangamutu near Pahiatua in the Wairarapa, into a family related to the secularist George Holyoake, and was raised in the Plymouth Brethren church. After his father's death he left school at 12 to work on the family hop and tobacco farm at Riwaka, his mother continuing his education at home. Farming associations drew him into politics: he won the Motueka seat for the Reform Party at a by-election in 1932, the youngest member of Parliament at 28, helped to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>, lost his seat in 1938 and returned for Pahiatua in 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidney-holland">Sidney Holland</a> he was Minister of Agriculture for eight years from 1949, encouraging farm mechanisation and a campaign that nearly eliminated the rabbit pest, and in 1954 became the first formally appointed Deputy Prime Minister. He succeeded Holland two months before the 1957 election, which Labour under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-nash">Walter Nash</a> won by two seats. He returned to power in 1960, campaigning against the Black Budget of 1958, and served as his own Minister of Foreign Affairs.</p><p>His government created the Office of the Ombudsman, passed the Crimes Act 1961 abolishing capital punishment, published the Hunn Report on the state of Māori, and introduced a form of voluntary unionism. It accepted a mixed economy and a long period of prosperity, negotiated a limited free trade agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in 1965, banned nuclear weapons from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> territory in 1963 and sent forces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, cautiously at first. After a narrow victory in 1969 the government came to seem careworn, and he resigned in 1972 in favour of Jack Marshall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1977 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-muldoon">Robert Muldoon</a>'s government had him appointed Governor-General, which opponents called a political appointment; Labour's leader Bill Rowling said he would remove him. His conduct was acknowledged to be impartial, and his term was limited to three years because of his age. He became a Knight of the Garter in 1980. Known as Kiwi Keith, he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> on 8 December 1983 and is the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> politician to have been both Prime Minister and Governor-General. He counted Kinloch, the town he developed on Lake Taupō, his proudest achievement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Keith Holyoake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Long periods of National Party rule under Sidney Holland and <b>Keith Holyoake</b> alternated with Labour governments under Walter Nash and Norman Kirk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pahiatua, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-40.45,175.84&amp;year=1955" aria-label="Pahiatua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Keith Holyoake"><image href="/maps/globes/g180s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4730 5894h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4730 5894h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-40.45,175.84&amp;year=1955">What was here in 1955? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
